<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Inner Niger Delta</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Niger Inland Delta, inland delta</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Inner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-delta">Niger Delta</a> is a vast seasonal floodplain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger River</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>. Its fertile land and waterways supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farming, fishing and trade, with cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djenne">Djenné</a>. It was a core economic region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/inner-niger-delta.jpg" alt="Inner Niger Delta" width="320" height="278" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Schmaltz, MODIS Land Rapid Response Team, NASA GSFC · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inland_Niger_Delta_2007.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Inner Niger Delta, also known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massina">Macina</a> or Masina, is an area of wetlands, lakes and floodplains in the semi-arid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a> of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, just south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. It consists of the middle course of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, the longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, between the bifurcated Niger and its tributary the Bani. The region is about 400 km long, and towns such as the river port of Mopti, Sévaré and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djenne">Djenné</a>, with its mud-brick Great Mosque, lie within it.</p><p>Rain falling in the upper basins of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> and Bani raises the water downstream, so flooding follows the rains with a delay. The wet season lasts from June to September, but the western and southern edges are not flooded until October. Only the lowest patches flood every year. In the dry season the area becomes a network of lakes and channels. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">Cattle</a>, pearl <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> are its main products, and it is a haven for large numbers of birds in the wet season.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The variable environment led the peoples of the delta to develop different traditional specialisations. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marka">Marka</a> cultivate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, the Bambara grow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">sorghum</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> are the leading herdsmen, and the Bozo and Somono the leading fishermen. According to the researchers who describe them, each group kept its own adaptations to the unpredictable floods secret from the others. The Fulani and Dogon inhabit the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massina">Macina</a> region and its surroundings.</p><p>In the early 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seku-amadu">Seku Amadu</a> founded a Massina Empire in the region and built a capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdullahi">Hamdullahi</a> in 1820. It fell in 1862 to the Toucouleur Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-hadj-umar-tall">El Hadj Umar Tall</a>, which in turn fell to the French army. The region became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> on its independence in 1960.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The delta lies in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> and has a population of over 500,000 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massina">Macina</a> region and surrounding area. Its vegetation is a green oasis in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a>, with aquatic grasses in the southern floodplains and palms on the sand ridges of the north. Hundreds of thousands of garganeys, pintails and ruffs winter there, and breeding colonies of cormorants, herons, spoonbills and ibises, along with the African manatee. Three Ramsar sites totalling 1,620 square kilometres have been declared: Lac Horo, Lac Debo and the Séri floodplain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Inner Niger Delta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His second was the trading city of Djenné in the <b>inland delta</b>, which, according to tradition, resisted the Siege of Djenné for seven years before submitting around 1473.</blockquote><a href="/empire/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They grew grain, above all millet, and cotton, working the land with iron tools made by the smiths, and raised sheep, goats and cattle; in the <b>inland delta</b> of the Niger the Bozo, Somono and Sorko lived by fishing, and Fulani herders kept cattle.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mali-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mali Empire</a></li></ul></section>
